<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bithynia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bithynian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bithynia is a historical region in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, bordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In the late thirteenth century its Byzantine borderlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> were the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a>. The region's position on the frontier between the Islamic and Byzantine worlds attracted ghazi warriors and made it the cradle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bithynia.jpg" alt="Bithynia" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caliniuc since Putzger &amp; Westermann atlases (Atlas zur Weltgeschichte, Stier, H.E., dir., 1985) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia_Minor_in_the_Greco-Roman_period_-_general_map_-_regions_and_main_settlements.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tribute paid to the Gauls of Tylis had strained its finances in the third century BCE, and the rise of <b>Bithynia</b> and Pergamon limited its room to manoeuvre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Pompey’s settlement joined western Pontus to <b>Bithynia</b> to form the province of Bithynia et Pontus and gave the rest to client rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>They were split into many tribes, about 200 of which have been identified, living across most of the Balkans, from the Getae north of the Danube to Pannonia in the west, and in <b>Bithynia</b> and Mysia in Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodore had gathered Byzantine refugees in <b>Bithynia</b> after the Fourth Crusade took Constantinople in 1204.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-15">15 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicomedes IV, restored to his throne by Rome, left <b>Bithynia</b> to the Roman Republic in his will in 74 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bithynia">Kingdom of Bithynia</a></li></ul></section>
